WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.438
Two timing facts shape the measurement.

2
00:00:02.438 --> 00:00:04.176
First, late entries.

3
00:00:04.176 --> 00:00:07.491
Learn says a scheduled workflow
still processes a user

4
00:00:07.491 --> 00:00:10.662
whose trigger date passed no
more than three days ago.

5
00:00:10.662 --> 00:00:14.237
Past three days, the scheduled
trigger skips the user.

6
00:00:14.237 --> 00:00:19.663
Book 03's Chapter 08 explains why a
separation entered late misses it.

7
00:00:19.663 --> 00:00:24.753
So rule S T three flags any separation
entered more than three days after its

8
00:00:24.753 --> 00:00:28.712
effective date: a Finding if
nobody ran the leaver on demand,

9
00:00:28.712 --> 00:00:30.651
and only a Warn if someone did.

10
00:00:30.651 --> 00:00:32.663
Second, the tenant's schedule.

11
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:37.801
Lifecycle Workflows runs on a schedule, and
that can delay a scheduled leaver's run.

12
00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.966
The wait is part of separation-to-block,

13
00:00:39.966 --> 00:00:43.825
so the guide says the schedule
belongs inside the approved interval.

14
00:00:43.825 --> 00:00:46.510
Set the threshold and
the schedule together.
